lineages={
1:'Direct historical authorial text in a 1912 translation/reprint; later editor notes distinguished. No new archival sample. Multhauf (IND-S02) also relies on Agricola, so these are not independent evidence for the same described pump.',
2:'Technical treatises (Agricola, Lohneyss, Calvör and later histories), mine-manager report transmitted through an 1898 edition, and mining coin imagery. Some lineage overlaps IND-S01; prior authors’ installation statements not directly re-audited.',
3:'Boulton/Watt and Cornish engineering histories, Lean/Pole duty reporting and reconstructed patents/ownership. Works by Hills, Barton, Rowe and von Tunzelmann mediate much evidence; the last also informs IND-S04. Not independent archival corroboration of all claims.',
4:'Reconstructed engine horsepower and costs from Kanefsky, Musson and von Tunzelmann, national accounts, and railway social savings. Some inherited engineering literature overlaps IND-S03. This analysis contributes accounting, not a new installation census.',
5:'New combination of terrain/historical climate, archival factory reports and a cartographic mill census. Historical interpretation also inherits earlier water/steam literature. Only specified text sections inspected; supplements and raw reconstruction not audited.',
6:'Abstract identifies Robert Thom and Lancashire reservoir proposals as historical material; exact archival and cost lineage unknown because the full article was not acquired.',
7:'Introductory argument summarising Allen’s larger wage, price and profitability work; underlying series not inspected in this excerpt. Shares a factor-price debate with IND-S08 and IND-S11, not independent corroboration of their figures.',
8:'New assembled archival/printed pay and productivity observations, including workhouses, putting-out accounts, schools and personal records; debates prior Muldrew/Allen estimates. Individual archive originals not directly inspected here.',
9:'Direct passages from Ure’s 1835 treatise transmitted by a sourcebook compiler; authorial views are evidence for advocacy. Ure also appears in IND-S08, so the shared quotation is not two independent observers.',
10:'Historical argument drawing on Musson/Robinson, Cookson, Rolt, Roe, biographies and contemporary engineering statements. Directly acquired paper, not original factory archives. Some engineering histories overlap other industrial sources.',
11:'Trade returns and wage observations inherited through historians including Chaudhuri, Wadsworth/Mann, Gilboy and Young; productivity partly inferred from price studies. Shares data and literature with IND-S12 and IND-S13; not three independent measurements of India’s decline.',
12:'Compiled relative prices, wages and drought/political histories; incorporates Broadberry/Gupta and earlier national/manufacturing reconstructions. Its model is new analysis of partly shared evidence, not independent observation of the same industrial-output ratios.',
13:'Author’s prior Atlantic trade research plus cited regional wage and colonial-trade studies; Charles Grant testimony via Chaudhuri. Substantial literature overlap with IND-S11/12. Claims about market formation are interpretation, not an independent experimental sample.',
14:'Newly analysed archived British machinery-supplier orders/specifications, plus installed-spindle compilations, technical manuals and worker histories. Relative performance uses proxies; observed orders and historical explanations have distinct evidential bases.',
15:'Pre-1949 section synthesises Feuerwerker, Xu/Min, Kraus, Fong, rural surveys and gender scholarship (Mann/Bray). Later archival/interview research is not projected backward. The reported 70% estimate is inherited, not newly measured here.',
16:'Contemporary engineering journals, manufacturers’ descriptions, technical textbooks and reconstructed census/capacity data (including DuBoff). Direct original articles generally not independently acquired. IND-S04 cites related electricity literature, so apparent agreement may share ancestors.'}
for s in sources:s['evidence_lineage']=lineages[int(s['id'][-2:])]

def test(n, chain, rival, discriminant, found, ambiguity, timing='emerged during research',status='underdetermined'):
    return dict(status=status,mechanism_chain=chain,strongest_rival=rival,discriminating_observation=discriminant,evidence_sought_found=found,remaining_ambiguity=ambiguity,hypothesis_timing=timing)
T={
2:test(2,'Remote hydraulic resource -> construct rod linkage -> mechanical power reaches distant pumps -> expanded site choice.', 'Static post-Agricola mining would predict little relevant configuration change; a weaker rival accepts change but denies cost advantage.', 'Later mechanisms and installations absent from Agricola distinguish change; operating accounts are needed for cost advantage.', 'Read Agricola and Multhauf (IND-S01/S02); latter compares remote rod/ balanced transmission and later depictions.', 'Configuration change is supported; diffusion rate and net advantage remain unresolved.',status='evaluated'),
3:test(3,'High fuel use plus local fuel price -> investor comparison -> fuel-saving design becomes more attractive -> Cornish deployment.', 'Drainage demand or capital/engineering availability alone could yield deployment without a coal-price mechanism.', 'Matched engine choices and total costs across sites with comparable drainage but different delivered coal prices.', 'Read IND-S03 pp. 10–12: coalfield/Cornish fuel contrast and condenser deployments; no matched-site experiment acquired.', 'Engine capability, finance and demand differ with geography.','pre-search candidate'),
4:test(4,'Comparable public engine reports -> engineer comparison and reputation -> incremental changes -> improved duty.', 'New high-pressure designs and investment alone predict improving duty, even without disclosure.', 'Within comparable investment/design cohorts, reporting exposure predicts improvement; evidence of actual copying strengthens transmission.', 'Read IND-S03 pp. 14–20: reporting intent, public comparisons, changing duty and shared ownership; original report series not audited.', 'Reporting, design, investment and district growth coincide.'),
5:test(5,'Broad patent enforcement -> restricted rival development -> delayed adoption of alternatives.', 'Patent rents finance development and disclosure; litigation may protect appropriation rather than reduce total innovation.', 'Blocked independently workable designs and post-restriction improvement controlling for engineering capacity; also evidence of R&D financed by rents.', 'Read IND-S03 pp. 12–14: Hornblower dispute, royalties, order cessation and subsequent slack period.', 'Dispute is stronger evidence than net innovation effect; expiry alone did not produce immediate progress.','pre-search candidate'),
7:test(7,'Local dependable water constraints -> mill seeks extra power -> steam supplement/replacement -> capacity expansion.', 'Labour access/control or agglomeration could favour steam despite adequate water; national abundance predicts no shortage only if local access is ignored.', 'Comparable mills/sites with usable flow versus scarce flow, plus seasonal output and expansion decisions.', 'Read IND-S05 impact/method sections: local Manchester constraint alongside national potential and supplementary steam; compared IND-S06 coordination abstract.', 'Industrial clustering partly creates scarcity; reservoir costs, rights and labour conditions unresolved.','pre-search candidate'),
8:test(8,'Shared reservoir requires funding and allocation -> manufacturers cannot coordinate -> proposed expansion not built.', 'Projects may be physically unreliable or financially unattractive even under feasible collective governance.', 'Scheme correspondence and cost/flow evidence showing viable projects blocked specifically by coordination, compared with realised schemes.', 'Searched full article; only IND-S06 author abstract acquired. It explicitly makes the coordination claim; scheme-level evidence not found.', 'Both rivals fit non-building. Weak-access claim retained only as attributed hypothesis.','pre-search candidate'),
9:test(9,'High relevant labour cost relative to machinery/energy -> expected cost saving -> investment in labour-saving inventions/adoption.', 'Low-paid spinning labour, quality requirements and skill/market complements could motivate or constrain adoption independently.', 'Task- and location-matched wages, output quality and machine payback comparisons before invention, including nonadopters.', 'Read Allen excerpt IND-S07 and archival-pay critique IND-S08; high-wage formulation is clear but spinner wage surge is challenged.', 'Allen’s underlying calculations and later responses not inspected; international and local wages use different comparisons.','pre-search candidate'),
10:test(10,'Employers extend low-paid labour supply and hold bargaining power -> spinners’ pay remains low -> presumed wage surge cannot explain timing.', 'Samples overrepresent institutional/part-time low-output labour, concealing a high-wage commercial cotton margin.', 'Comparable full-time commercial cotton spinning records with counts and hours, versus institutional and other fibres.', 'Read IND-S08 source definitions, records and regressions: no general surge, source-type gaps, sparse cotton observations; explicit limits preserved.', 'A changed marginal opportunity cost may differ from typical average earnings; pre-factory cotton evidence limited.','emerged during research'),
12:test(12,'Precision craft/measurement capabilities -> reproducible complicated components -> machinery supply and replacement -> sustained industrial use.', 'Demand/capital expansion alone induces both tool skill and machinery; capabilities could be an outcome rather than prior constraint.', 'Dated personnel/tool transfers preceding new workable designs and matched sites with demand but missing skills.', 'Read IND-S10 sections 2.1–2.3: named instrument/mechanic transfers, valve making and machine-tool/parts account; also simpler early artifacts acknowledged.', 'Demand and supply reinforce one another; broad national claims lack an exogenous comparison.','pre-search candidate'),
13:test(13,'Productivity rises unevenly and cotton/freight costs change -> relative delivered cost shifts -> merchants source differently -> competitive reversal.', 'Colonial policy and coercive market access could drive the same trade shifts without equivalent cost advantage.', 'Quality-matched delivered costs and shares across markets with different policy but similar transport, before and after price changes.', 'Read IND-S11 pp. 16–25 and conclusion; compared IND-S13 policy testimony. The cost model includes cotton/freight but assumes some Indian productivity paths.', 'Policy shapes prices and markets; quality and labour-unit comparability remain incomplete.','pre-search candidate'),
14:test(14,'Domestic agricultural/political shock -> dear grain and subsistence-linked nominal wages -> reduced textile competitiveness; later world textile prices add pressure.', 'Colonial interventions or textile demand shifts could alter prices and employment without the posited agricultural cause.', 'District timing: independently measured harvest/drought/conflict shocks preceding wage pressure and textile contraction, including low-shock controls.', 'Read IND-S12 model and relative-price/timing argument; no independently identified district treatment comparison found.', 'Modelled labour mobility and wage linkage do causal work; price data alone fit multiple mechanisms.','emerged during research'),
15:test(15,'Slavery-linked Atlantic commodity trade -> expanding markets -> regional protoindustry growth -> investment and mechanisation incentives.', 'Domestic markets, factor prices or engineering can explain innovation; Atlantic demand might follow rather than precede industrial advantage.', 'Regional/sectoral demand exposure preceding investment, with comparable low-exposure producers and separate supply/finance channels.', 'Read IND-S13 regional wage/exports sequence and IND-S11 competition account; temporal market linkage found, no isolated necessity test.', 'Atlantic markets, shipping, wages and political power are reciprocally connected; export shares are not investment causality.','pre-search candidate'),
16:test(16,'Imperial tariff authority -> asymmetric protection/import permission -> altered producer choice set and market access.', 'Productivity and freight advantages alone would produce the same import shift under symmetric sovereignty.', 'Comparable products under differing tariff regimes and original legislative/merchant records on excluded choices.', 'Read Grant testimony as reproduced in IND-S13 section IV; sought original-record specificity but did not acquire parliamentary source.', 'Testimony establishes awareness and constrained sovereignty more firmly than the counterfactual trade magnitude.','pre-search candidate'),
17:test(17,'Imported fibre variety plus labour-intensive mixing -> reduced breakage/broader fibre control -> viable rings under Japanese conditions.', 'Changes in machine design or yarn demand alone could improve performance without preparation reorganisation.', 'Within comparable machinery and yarn counts, mixing adoption precedes improved throughput/breakage; costs and work hours measured.', 'Read IND-S14 pp. 549–551, order table and contemporary observations: timing of mixed-cotton orders and package change support the pathway.', 'Order proxies do not independently identify labour, fibres and design; exact realised productivity not observed.','emerged during research'),
18:test(18,'Mule-specific skills and continuing machine improvement -> sustained capability -> continued purchases where advantageous.', 'Entrenched employers/unions or sunk capital produce persistence despite inferior returns.', 'Compare realised quality-adjusted profits and best-practice improvements in new mule purchases versus feasible rings, not only old stocks.', 'Read IND-S14 performance specifications and choice model: both technologies improve before 1914 and multiple countries buy mules.', 'New orders weaken a pure obsolete-stock story but may still reflect expectations, omitted costs or institutional constraints.','pre-search candidate'),
20:test(20,'Cheap machine yarn -> replace selected spinning inputs -> handloom production retains product functions and expands accessible supply.', 'General market growth alone may sustain hand weaving; poor households may simply be unable to buy cloth.', 'Yarn-price/access changes preceding weaving expansion, with product-level evidence of mixed warp/weft and comparison to inaccessible districts.', 'Read IND-S15 pp. 373–374: explicit mixed yarn and named new weaving districts; original local studies not independently acquired.', 'Price, population, cash and fabric differences interact; mechanism stronger than national quantitative effect.','emerged during research'),
24:test(24,'Compatible motors/tools plus redesign and service -> flexible local power -> productive reorganisation -> adoption benefits.', 'Demand-led expansion or new capital generally causes both redesign and productivity, with motors only accompanying it.', 'Matched retrofits/new plants measuring workflow and output before/after, with comparable non-electric new equipment.', 'Read IND-S16 configuration history, redesign comments and utility installation services; evidence for needed complements found, no clean experiment.', 'Selective engineering cases and firm growth can bias productivity interpretation; unit drive not always superior.','pre-search candidate')
}
for c in claims:
    n=int(c['id'][-2:])
    if c['causal_status']=='descriptive':
        c['test']={'status':'descriptive-only','reason':'The bounded claim identifies a text, configuration, attributed estimate or observed dataset measure; it does not estimate the causes of the wider outcome. See inference_limits for the distinction.'}
    else:
        assert n in T,(n,c['causal_status']);c['test']=T[n]

# One weak abstract-only dependency is explicitly downgraded; no bounded necessity is established.
for e in graph['edges']:
    if e['relation']=='depends-on':e['relation']='complements'
config={1:'Direct/remote mine drainage under site-specific water and transmission conditions',2:'Cornish pumping engineering and investor arrangements in the specified phase',3:'British textile-mill power choices, including unrealised collective reservoirs',4:'Mechanised spinning and reproducible machine supply in British textile districts',5:'Quality- and market-specific India–Lancashire competition',6:'Ring/mule orders and local production packages, 1878–1933',7:'Selected Chinese household yarn/weaving configurations before 1949',8:'US factory mechanical-drive configurations and electricity provision'}
alt={
1:['Human or animal pumping','Drainage works','Foregoing deeper extraction'],2:['Different engine designs','Hydraulic power where suitable','Investment and engineering without public reporting'],
3:['Existing or relocated waterpower','Individual steam','Mixed power','Reduced expansion'],4:['Outwork','Imported yarn','Simpler hand machinery','Other craft-supply routes'],
5:['Different products or markets','Different trade policy where politically possible','Domestic mechanisation'],6:['Mules','Rings with other fibres or preparation','Retaining existing machines'],
7:['Hand-spun inputs','Purchased factory cloth','Other household work where available'],8:['Group drive','Line shafts','Onsite generation','Utility supply']}
for e in graph['edges']:
    n=int(e['id'][-2:]);c=int(e['case_ids'][0][-2:])
    e['basis']='observed-contribution'
    if n in [1,2,3]: e['basis']='technical-possibility'
    if n in [8,12,25]:e['basis']='hypothesis'
    if n==11:e['basis']='hypothesis'
    e['scope']={'case_id':e['case_ids'][0],'stage':('non-adoption/proposed expansion' if n in [11,12] else 'deployment and use' if c in [1,7,8] else 'diffusion and improvement'),'configuration':config[c]+'; '+e['qualification']}
    e['alternatives']=alt[c]
    e['joint_inputs']=[]
    e['necessity_rationale']='No necessity claim'
    e['dispute']=e['qualification']
# Keep local water configurations distinct. The same abstract resource category
# does not make sixteenth-century mine hydraulics identical to British mill power.
